## Why I made this

Before this course, I never really thought about social media as something that needed rules or habits. I just used it. But after reading a few case studies about people losing job offers or getting into trouble over old posts, it hit me how permanent everything online actually is.

This checklist is something I made for myself as much as for this assignment.

---

## Before posting anything

- [ ] Would I be okay if my professor, a future employer, or my parents saw this?
- [ ] Am I sharing something I've actually verified, or am I just reacting?
- [ ] Does this include someone else's details or photos without their knowledge?
- [ ] Am I posting this because it's worth sharing, or just because I'm bored or emotional right now?
- [ ] Is the tone right for the platform? (LinkedIn ≠ Instagram ≠ Twitter)

---

## Account security

- [ ] Strong, unique password — not the same one I use elsewhere
- [ ] Two-factor authentication turned on
- [ ] Checked which third-party apps have access to my account (removed ones I don't use)
- [ ] Phone number and email for recovery are up to date
- [ ] Gone through tagged photos and untagged anything I wouldn't want showing up

---

## Professional profiles (LinkedIn / GitHub)

- [ ] Profile photo is clear and looks like me — not a blurry selfie
- [ ] Headline says something meaningful, not just "Student at VIT Bhopal"
- [ ] About/bio section is written and not left blank
- [ ] Education filled in properly
- [ ] Skills listed reflect what I can actually do — no exaggerating
- [ ] At least one project or post is visible
- [ ] Connection requests go out with a short note, not just the default

---

## Things to avoid

- [ ] Don't post your real-time location publicly
- [ ] Don't screenshot and share private conversations
- [ ] Don't post anything confidential — about college, exams, peers, institutions
- [ ] Don't send or reply to messages when you're angry — write it, wait, then decide
- [ ] Don't click links in DMs even from people you know — their account might be compromised
- [ ] Never share an OTP, UPI PIN, or password on any platform, ever
- [ ] Avoid getting into public arguments — even if you're right, it rarely ends well

---

## Things to check every few months

- [ ] Look back at recent posts — do they still represent you well?
- [ ] Google your own name and see what comes up
- [ ] Review privacy settings — platforms update their defaults without telling you
- [ ] Update your profile if something significant has changed
- [ ] Archive anything old that you wouldn't post today

---

## The one thing that stuck with me

Someone in class mentioned that your digital footprint is basically a version of you that exists 24/7, even when you're offline. Anyone can look you up at any time. That framing changed how I think about posting. It's not about paranoia — it's just about being intentional.

---

> *"Post like someone is always watching — because with a public profile, they are."*
